<chapter id="hashtable">
#title("Linear Hashtables")

#para("\

Hashtables are commonly used to implement finite maps. In ATSLIB/libats,
there are hashtable implementations based on linear chaining
(#myatscodelink("libats/SATS/hashtbl_chain.sats", "libats/SATS/hashtbl_chain.sats"))
and linear probing
(#myatscodelink("libats/SATS/hashtbl_linprb.sats", "libats/SATS/hashtbl_linprb.sats")).
The hashtables of both are #emphasis("linear"): a hashtable is
created by the programmer and it must be freed by the programmer, which
the type-checker enforces. (The persistent hashtables, which can only be
reclaimed through garbage collection, and the wrapper
#filename("libats/ML/HATS/myhashtblref.hats") that made them easy to use are
not available any more together with the support for garbage collection.)
In this chapter, I show how a linear hashtable can be created and operated
on.\

")

#para('\

Suppose that a map is needed for mapping keys of type
#stacode("key") to items of type #stacode("itm"). The following code
essentially sets up the interface and the implementation of a map based on
the hashtable of ATSLIB/libats that uses linear chaining. Notice that the
implementation needs a hash function and the implementation of
the list-based map for the buckets:

#dats2xhtml('\
staload "libats/SATS/hashtbl_chain.sats"
//
staload _(*anon*) = "libats/DATS/hashfun.dats"
staload _(*anon*) = "libats/DATS/linmap_list.dats"
staload _(*anon*) = "libats/DATS/qlist.dats"
staload _(*anon*) = "libats/DATS/hashtbl_chain.dats"
//
typedef key = string and itm = int
')

')

#para('\

Assume that #stacode("key") is #stacode("string") and #stacode("itm")
is #stacode("int"). The following line of code creates a hashtable with its
initial capacity set to be 1000:

#dats2xhtml('\
val mymap = hashtbl_make_nil<key,itm>(i2sz(1000))
')

Note that the capacity in this case is the size of the array associated
with the created hashtable. The underlying hashtable implementation is
based on linear chaining, and this hashtable can store up to 5000 (5*1000)
items without need for resizing. When resizing is indeed needed, it is done
automatically. The value #dyncode("mymap") is linear: it has to be
freed by calling #dyncode("hashtbl_free") (see below). The following two
lines of code show the number of items and the capacity of the hashtable:

#dats2xhtml('\
val () =
fprintln! (stdout_ref, "mymap.size = ", hashtbl_get_size(mymap))
val () =
fprintln! (stdout_ref, "mymap.capacity = ", hashtbl_get_capacity(mymap))
')

The following few lines insert some key/item pairs into
#dyncode("mymap"):

#dats2xhtml('\
val-~None_vt() = hashtbl_insert_opt(mymap, "a", 0)
val-~Some_vt(0) = hashtbl_insert_opt(mymap, "a", 1)
//
val-~None_vt() = hashtbl_insert_opt(mymap, "b", 1)
val-~Some_vt(1) = hashtbl_insert_opt(mymap, "b", 2)
//
val-~None_vt() = hashtbl_insert_opt(mymap, "c", 2)
val-~Some_vt(2) = hashtbl_insert_opt(mymap, "c", 3)
')

Given a key and an item, #dyncode("hashtbl_insert_opt") inserts the
key/item pair into #dyncode("mymap").
If the key is in the domain of the map represented by #dyncode("mymap")
before insertion, then the original item associated with the key is
returned (as the value of #dyncode("Some_vt"), a linear option). Otherwise,
#dyncode("None_vt") is returned. The patterns
#dyncode("val-~None_vt()") and #dyncode("val-~Some_vt(2)") free the
returned option while they match it. As can be expected, the size
of #dyncode("mymap") is 3 at this point:

#dats2xhtml('\
val () = assertloc (hashtbl_get_size(mymap) = 3)
val () = fprintln! (stdout_ref, "mymap = ", mymap)
')

The function #dyncode("hashtbl_get_size") returns the number of key/item
pairs stored in a given hashtable.  During the course of debugging, one may
want to print out the key/item pairs in a given hashtable; the symbol
#dyncode("fprint") is overloaded with #dyncode("fprint_hashtbl"). The next
two lines of code show how search with a given key operates on a hashtable:

#dats2xhtml('\
val-~None_vt() = hashtbl_search_opt(mymap, "")
val-~Some_vt(1) = hashtbl_search_opt(mymap, "a")
')

The function #dyncode("hashtbl_search_opt") returns the item associated with
a given key if it is found. The next few lines of code remove some key/item
pairs from #dyncode("mymap"):

#dats2xhtml('\
val-true = hashtbl_remove(mymap, "a")
val-false = hashtbl_remove(mymap, "a")
//
val-~Some_vt(2) = hashtbl_takeout_opt(mymap, "b")
val-~Some_vt(3) = hashtbl_takeout_opt(mymap, "c")
')

The function #dyncode("hashtbl_remove") removes the key/item pair of a given
key. If a key/item pair is removed, then the function returns
true. Otherwise, it returns false to indicates that no key/item pair of the
given key is stored in the hashtable being operated on.  The function
#dyncode("hashtbl_takeout_opt") is similar to
#dyncode("hashtbl_remove") excepting for returning the removed item.
The next few lines of code make use of several less commonly used functions
on hashtables:

#dats2xhtml('\
val () = hashtbl_insert_any(mymap, "a", 0)
val () = hashtbl_insert_any(mymap, "b", 1)
val () = hashtbl_insert_any(mymap, "c", 2)
val kxs = hashtbl_listize1(mymap)
val ((*void*)) = fprintln! (stdout_ref, "kxs = ", kxs)
val ((*void*)) = list_vt_free<(key,itm)>(kxs)
val kxs = hashtbl_takeout_all(mymap)
val ((*void*)) = fprintln! (stdout_ref, "kxs = ", kxs)
val ((*void*)) = list_vt_free<(key,itm)>(kxs)
')

The function #dyncode("hashtbl_insert_any") #emphasis("always") inserts a
given key/item pair regardless whether the key is already in use. One
should really avoid using this function or only call it when it is
absolutely sure that the given key is not already in use for otherwise the
involved hashtable would be corrupted.  The functions
#dyncode("hashtbl_listize1") and #dyncode("hashtbl_takeout_all") return a
linear list consisting of all the key/item pairs in a given hashtable; the
former keeps the hashtable unchanged while the latter empties it. The list
is linear, too, and it is freed by #dyncode("list_vt_free"). Last, I present
as follows the interface for an iterator going over all the key/item pairs
in a given hashtable:

#sats2xhtml('\
fun
{key:t0p
;itm:vt0p}
hashtbl_foreach(tbl: !hashtbl(key, INV(itm))): void
//
fun
{key:t0p
;itm:vt0p}
{env:vt0p}
hashtbl_foreach$fwork(k: key, x: &itm >> _, &env >> _): void
')

As an example, the following code prints out all the key/item
pairs in a given hashtable: the template
#dyncode("hashtbl_foreach$fwork") is implemented to specify the work to
do for each of them (there is no closure-function to create).

#dats2xhtml('\
val () = let
//
implement
hashtbl_foreach$fwork<key,itm><void>
  (k, x, env) = fprintln! (stdout_ref, "k=", k, " and ", "x=", x)
//
in
  hashtbl_foreach<key,itm> (mymap)
end // end of [val]
')

Finally, the hashtable is freed:

#dats2xhtml('\
val () = hashtbl_free (mymap)
')

')

#para("\

Please find the entirety of the code used in this chapter
#mycodelink("chap_hashtable.dats", "on-line"). Also, there is a
hashtable-based implementation of symbol table available
#mycodelink("chap_hashtable2.dats", "on-line"): since the table lives as
long as the program, it is created once and never freed, and the (unsafe)
casts in the code only pass the table around without consuming it.

")#comment("para")

</chapter><!--"hashtable"-->

